-author of the research on tolvaptan in chronic SIADH treatment.SANTIAGO A:Santiago A is a co-author involved in the study on tolvaptan.NUTRICI√ìN PEDI√ÅTRICA:NUTRICI√ìN PEDI√ÅTRICA is a specialized field of medicine dedicated to addressing the nutritional needs and dietary management of infants and children. This area encompasses a comprehensive range of topics, including dietary recommendations for various age groups, focusing on essential macronutrients and micronutrients necessary for healthy growth and development. 

The guidelines within NUTRICI√ìN PEDI√ÅTRICA emphasize the importance of appropriate sodium intake, milk consumption, and the gradual introduction of diverse foods to stimulate appetite and meet nutritional requirements. It also addresses the energy needs and nutrient intake during different stages of childhood, ensuring that dietary plans are tailored to support both undernutrition and overnutrition.

Furthermore, NUTRICI√ìN PEDI√ÅTRICA includes nutritional assessments and interventions aimed at promoting optimal growth and health in pediatric patients. It provides specific dietary guidelines for critical conditions, focusing on medical nutrition therapy and the administration of parenteral nutrition for those with particular medical needs. The field also highlights the significance of enteral nutrition as the preferred method for delivering nutrients to children, ensuring that their dietary needs are met effectively.

Overall, NUTRICI√ìN PEDI√ÅTRICA is essential for guiding healthcare professionals in the nutritional support and dietary management of infants, children, and adolescents, ultimately promoting their health and development.CONIVAPTAN:Conivaptan is a medication used for the treatment of hyponatremia, particularly in patients with euvolemic hyponatremia.TOLVAPTAN:CL√çNICO UNIVERSITARIO VALENCIA:Cl√≠nico Universitario Valencia is an institution focused on clinical nutrition and diet therapy, providing educational resources and research in the field of pediatric nutrition.TRASTORNOS NUTRICIONALES:Trastornos Nutricionales refers to nutritional disorders that can affect children, which are addressed through clinical evaluation and intervention.DESNUTRICI√ìN AGUDA:Desnutrici√≥n Aguda is a condition characterized by a rapid loss of weight and body mass due to inadequate nutrition, particularly relevant in pediatric care.DESNUTRICI√ìN CR√ìNICA:Desnutrici√≥n Cr√≥nica refers to long-term undernutrition that affects growth and development in children.PEDIATRIC PATIENT:Pediatric patients are children who are assessed for their nutritional status and health, with a particular focus on their growth and dietary intake.TANNER STAGES:The Tanner Stages are a classification system used to assess the stages of pubertal development in children.UNDERNUTRITION:Undernutrition is a condition resulting from insufficient nutrient intake, leading to health problems such as stunted growth in children.CLINICAL EXAMINATION:Clinical examination is a systematic approach to assess a patient's health, particularly focusing on physical signs of nutritional status in children.HEALTH RECORD:Health records are documents that contain information about a patient's medical history, including dietary habits and growth patterns.CHILD:Children are individuals in the developmental stage of life, typically under the age of 18, who are assessed for health and nutritional status.NUTRITIONAL DISORDER:ADULTS:Adults are individuals who have reached maturity and have stable body mass compared to children.HEIGHT:Height is a fundamental anthropometric measure that evaluates the length or stature of an individual, important for growth assessment.HEAD CIRCUMFERENCE:Head circumference is a measurement used to assess brain development and growth in infants and young children.ARM CIRCUMFERENCE:Arm circumference is a measurement that helps evaluate body composition and nutritional status, particularly in children.TRICEP SKINFOLD:Tricep skinfold measurement assesses subcutaneous fat and helps evaluate body fat percentage and nutritional status.SUBSCAPULAR SKINFOLD:Subscapular skinfold measurement is used to assess body fat distribution and overall nutritional status.WEIGHT:ORBEGOZO:Orbegozo is an organization that published growth reference patterns for children in Spain, with editions dating back to 1988.CARRASCOSA ET AL.:Carrascosa et al. is a research group that conducted a study highlighting the obesity trend among Spanish children.EURO-GROWTH 2000:Euro-Growth 2000 is a multicentric study that established growth patterns for European children aged 0-5 years.NCHS:The National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS) is an organization that provides data on the growth and nutrition of school-aged children and adolescents in the United States. However, it is important to note that the NCHS was replaced by the CDC's 2000 growth standards in the United States, indicating a shift in the authority and standards used for assessing growth and nutrition in this demographic.BRAZIL:Brazil is one of the countries included in the WHO's international growth reference patterns for children.INDIA:India is a country that plays a significant role in providing data on child growth and nutrition within the context of the World Health Organization (WHO). It is also included in the WHO's international growth reference patterns for children, contributing to global standards and understanding of child health and development.GHANA:Ghana is a country that plays a significant role in providing data on child growth and nutrition within the context of the World Health Organization (WHO). It is included in the WHO's international growth reference patterns for children, which contributes to the global understanding of child growth. Through its participation, Ghana aids in the development of comprehensive insights into the nutritional status and growth patterns of children, thereby supporting global health initiatives.OMAN:Oman is included in the WHO's international growth reference patterns for children, reflecting diverse socioeconomic conditions.NORUEGA:Noruega es un pa√≠s que proporciona datos sobre el crecimiento infantil y nutrici√≥n en el contexto de la OMS.OM√ÅN:Om√°n es un pa√≠s que proporciona datos sobre el crecimiento infantil y nutrici√≥n en el contexto de la OMS.PATRONES PERCENTILADOS:Los patrones percentilados son herramientas utilizadas para evaluar el crecimiento y el estado nutricional de los ni√±os en comparaci√≥n con est√°ndares de referencia.GURNEY AND JELLIFFE:Gurney and Jelliffe are referenced for their nomogram used to calculate muscle area in nutritional assessments.HEMATOL√ìGIA:Hematology is a field of study that focuses on blood disorders, including anemia associated with malnutrition.ALBUMINA S√âRICA:Serum albumin is a protein that reflects the nutritional status and synthesis capacity of the liver.ANEMIA CARENCIAL:Deficiency anemia is a condition that occurs when the body does not have enough healthy red blood cells due to a lack of iron, which is often associated with malnutrition.DESNUTRICI√ìN AGUDA MODERADA:Moderate acute malnutrition is a condition characterized by a significant weight loss or low weight-for-height in children, indicating a serious health risk.DESNUTRICI√ìN AGUDA GRAVE:Severe acute malnutrition is a life-threatening condition that occurs when a child is extremely thin for their height or has nutritional edema.DESNUTRICI√ìN CR√ìNICA MODERADA:Moderate chronic malnutrition is characterized by low height-for-age, indicating long-term nutritional deficiencies.DESNUTRICI√ìN CR√ìNICA GRAVE:Severe chronic malnutrition is a serious condition where a child is significantly shorter than the average for their age due to prolonged nutritional deficiencies.√çNDICES ERITROCITARIOS:Erythrocyte indices are measurements used to evaluate the size and hemoglobin content of red blood cells, which can indicate nutritional status.RECUENTO DE RETICULOCITOS:Reticulocyte count is a test that measures the number of young red blood cells in the blood, which can help assess bone marrow function and response to anemia.MART√çNEZ COSTA C.:Mart√≠nez Costa C. is an author who contributed to the bibliography on the assessment of nutritional status in children.ARGUELLES MART√çN F.:Arguelles Mart√≠n F. is an author who contributed to the bibliography on the assessment of nutritional status in children.GARC√çA NOVO MD:Garc√≠a Novo MD is a notable author and editor in the field of pediatric nutrition. He has contributed significantly to the bibliography on the assessment of nutritional status in children, enhancing the understanding and evaluation of children's dietary needs. Additionally, Garc√≠a Novo MD plays a crucial role as an editor in the publication of pediatric nutrition guidelines, further establishing his expertise and influence in this important area of healthcare.PAV√ìN:Pav√≥n is an author who contributed to the bibliography on the assessment of nutritional status in children.IMPIDANCIA BIOEL√âCTRICA (BIA):Bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA) is a technique used to assess body composition, particularly in children.DENSITOMETR√çA (DXA):Densitometry (DXA) is an examination that quantifies bone mineral content, important for children with dietary deficiencies.RADIOGRAF√çA DE CARPO:Wrist radiography is an essential examination for assessing skeletal maturation in children with growth delays.MALNUTRICI√ìN CR√ìNICA:Chronic malnutrition is a condition often associated with systemic diseases, leading to delays in skeletal maturation.C-REACTIVA PROTE√çNA:BILBAO:Bilbao is a city in Spain where a growth study was conducted.GROWTH STANDARDS:Growth standards refer to the benchmarks established for assessing the growth and development of children.PAV√ìN RELINCH√ìN P:Pav√≥n Relinch√≥n P is an editor who contributed to the Tratado de Gastroenterolog√≠a, Hepatolog√≠a y Nutrici√≥n Pedi√°trica.ROM√ÅN RIECHMAN E:Rom√°n Riechman E is an editor associated with the Tratado de Gastroenterolog√≠a, Hepatolog√≠a y Nutrici√≥n Pedi√°trica.SILVA GARC√çA G:Silva Garc√≠a G is an editor who contributed to the Tratado de Gastroenterolog√≠a, Hepatolog√≠a y Nutrici√≥n Pedi√°trica.SOJO AGUIRRE A:Sojo Aguirre A is an editor involved in the publication of pediatric nutrition guidelines.ERGO:ERGO is a publishing house known for its contributions to the field of pediatric nutrition. It has published several works on this subject, including a comprehensive manual specifically focused on pediatric nutrition.FUNDACI√ìN FAUSTINO ORBEGOZO:Fundaci√≥n Faustino Orbegozo is an organization that conducted a growth study in Bilbao.AN PEDIATR (BARC):An Pediatr (Barc) is a journal that publishes articles related to pediatric health and nutrition.ARGUELLES MART√çN F:SUSANA REDECILLAS FERREIRO:Susana Redecillas Ferreiro is a medical professional involved in pediatric nutrition and diet therapy.RAQUEL N√ö√ëEZ RAMOS:Raquel N√∫√±ez Ramos is a medical professional specializing in pediatric nutrition.RAQUEL LORITE CUENCA:Raquel Lorite Cuenca is a medical professional working in the field of pediatric gastroenterology and nutrition.UNU:The United Nations University (UNU) is an international community of scholars dedicated to addressing global issues through research, capacity development, and the dissemination of knowledge. As an academic and research institution, UNU focuses on conducting research and providing education that contributes to the understanding and resolution of pressing global challenges.ACADEMIA DE CIENCIAS AMERICANA:The American Academy of Sciences is a prestigious organization that promotes scientific research and education in the United States.LACANTES:Infants, specifically those aged 0-1 year, who have specific nutritional requirements for healthy growth and development.TEMA 53:TEMA 53 focuses on dietary recommendations specifically tailored for pediatric patients, emphasizing their unique nutritional needs and the importance of metabolic calculations. This entity provides guidance aimed at ensuring that the dietary requirements of children are adequately met, taking into account their growth and developmental stages.SCHOFIELD:The term "Schofield" refers to a set of equations utilized for estimating basal metabolic expenditure, particularly in children, by taking into account their weight and height. These equations are significant in the context of predicting energy requirements for patients, especially pediatric populations. The Schofield equations provide a valuable tool for healthcare professionals in assessing the metabolic needs of children, ensuring appropriate nutritional support and energy balance.NI√ëAS:Ni√±as refers to girls, specifically in the context of dietary recommendations and nutritional needs. The energy requirements for this demographic are being calculated in the associated table, highlighting the importance of understanding their nutritional needs.FACTOR DE MULTIPLICACI√ìN:Multiplication factors are used to adjust energy expenditure based on clinical situations in pediatric patients.CIRUG√çA MAYOR:Major surgery is a clinical situation that influences energy expenditure in pediatric patients.SEDACI√ìN:Sedation is a clinical situation that can decrease energy expenditure in pediatric patients.GASTO METAB√ìLICO BASAL:CANTIDAD:Cantidad refers to the amount of nutrients required in the diet, which is critical for meeting the nutritional needs of children.ACTIVIDAD F√çSICA LIGERA:Light physical activity level as categorized in the energy requirements table.ACTIVIDAD F√çSICA MODERADA:Moderate physical activity level as categorized in the energy requirements table.ACTIVIDAD F√çSICA INTENSA:Intense physical activity level as categorized in the energy requirements table.KCAL/D√çA:Kcal/d√≠a indicates the daily caloric intake required for girls based on their age and activity level.KCAL/KG/D:Kcal/kg/d represents the caloric intake per kilogram of body weight for girls based on their age and activity level.DIETARY REFERENCE INTAKE:The Dietary Reference Intake (DRI) provides guidelines for nutrient intake levels to meet the needs of most healthy individuals.HOLLIDAY-SEGAR EQUATION:The Holliday-Segar equation is a formula used to calculate fluid requirements for children based on their weight.TABLE 7:Table 7 provides adequate water intake recommendations for children of various ages.TABLE 8:Table 8 details protein requirements for children based on age.TABLE 9:Table 9 discusses the composition of dietary fats and recommendations for fat intake in children.MOTHER'S MILK:Mother's milk is the primary source of nutrition for infants in the first six months, providing essential lipids and fatty acids.LIQUID INTAKE RECOMMENDATIONS:Liquid intake recommendations provide guidelines for the amount of fluid children should consume based on their age.PROTEIN REQUIREMENTS:Protein requirements outline the necessary daily intake of protein for children based on their age and development.NUTRIENT INTAKE:Nutrient intake encompasses the overall consumption of essential vitamins and minerals necessary for a child's growth and health.AAP:AAP is the American Academy of Pediatrics, which offers recommendations and guidelines for pediatric health and nutrition.BOLLER√çA INDUSTRIAL:BOLLER√çA INDUSTRIAL refers to industrially produced baked goods that are often high in sugars and fats, and should be limited in children's diets.AGPI N-6:AGPI n-6 refers to omega-6 fatty acids that are essential for health and should be included in children's diets.AGPI N-3:AGPI n-3 refers to omega-3 fatty acids that are crucial for brain development and overall health in children.NECESIDADES DE MICRONUTRIENTES:NECESIDADES DE MICRONUTRIENTES refers to the essential vitamins and minerals required for children's health.RECOMENDACIONES DE LA ESPGHAN:RECOMENDACIONES DE LA ESPGHAN are specific dietary guidelines provided by the European Society for Pediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition.RECOMENDACIONES DE LA AAP:RECOMENDACIONES DE LA AAP are specific dietary guidelines provided by the American Academy of Pediatrics.LEGISLACI√ìN DE LA UNI√ìN EUROPEA:LEGISLACI√ìN DE LA UNI√ìN EUROPEA refers to the laws and regulations set by the European Union that impact nutrition guidelines.)<|COMPLETE|>CEREALES RICOS EN FIBRA:Cereales ricos en fibra are high-fiber cereals that contribute to dietary fiber intake.PAN BLANCO:Pan blanco is a type of bread that is low in fiber content.PAN DE MOLDE:Pan de molde is a type of bread that is commonly used for sandwiches and has moderate fiber content.ESPINACAS CONGELADAS:Espinacas congeladas are frozen spinach, a vegetable rich in fiber.GUISANTES CONGELADOS:Guisantes congelados are frozen peas, which are a good source of dietary fiber.LENTEJAS:Lentejas, commonly known as lentils, are legumes that serve as a nutritious food source. They provide 7.1 mg of iron per 100 grams, making them a valuable addition to a diet, particularly for those needing to increase their iron intake. Additionally, lentejas are recognized for being a good source of dietary fiber, contributing to digestive health and overall well-being.ALBARICOQUES:Albaricoques are apricots, a fruit that contains dietary fiber.CEREZAS:Cerezas are cherries, a fruit that provides some dietary fiber.CIRUELAS:Ciruelas are plums that are recognized for their nutritional benefits, providing 3 mg of iron per 100 grams. Additionally, they are known for their high fiber content, making them a healthy choice for those looking to improve their dietary intake.LECHE HUMANA:Human milk is the ideal exclusive food for infants in the first 6 months of life, providing optimal calcium and nutrientsVERDURAS DE HOJA VERDE:Leafy green vegetables are a good source of calcium, particularly those low in oxalates to enhance absorption6-12 MESES:Age group for infants where specific dietary recommendations for calcium and iron are provided1-3 A√ëOS:The entity "1-3 A√ëOS" refers to the age group of toddlers, specifically those aged between one to three years. This age group is associated with specific dietary recommendations, particularly concerning the recommended dietary allowances for essential nutrients such as calcium and iron. These guidelines are crucial for supporting the growth and development of children in this formative stage.4-8 A√ëOS:The entity "4-8 a√±os" refers to the age group of young children, specifically those aged 4 to 8 years old. This age group is significant for various health and demographic statistics, as it encompasses important developmental milestones. There are specific dietary recommendations associated with this age range, particularly concerning calcium intake, which is crucial for healthy growth and development. Additionally, this group is categorized for health metrics and developmental indicators, highlighting the importance of monitoring their well-being during these formative years.9-18 A√ëOS:Age group for adolescents with increased dietary needs for calcium and ironANEMIA FISIOL√ìGICA:Physiological anemia in infants that occurs due to changes in hemoglobin synthesis at birth0-6 MESES:The term "0-6 meses" refers to the age group of infants from birth to 6 months. This period is characterized by specific dietary recommendations tailored to the nutritional needs of infants during their early development.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN CALCIO:Foods rich in calcium are recommended for maintaining adequate calcium levels in the body, especially in children and adolescents.ADOLESCENTES:Adolescents experience increased nutritional demands, particularly for iron and calcium, due to growth spurts.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN HIERRO:Iron-rich foods are recommended for adolescents to prevent iron deficiency and support growth.ALMEJAS:Almejas are a type of shellfish that are rich in iron, providing 24 mg of iron per 100 g.PESCADILLA:Pescadilla is a type of fish that contains 11-13 mg of iron per 100 g.OSTRAS:Ostras are shellfish that provide 6.5 mg of iron per 100 g.MEJILLONES:Mejillones are a type of shellfish that contain 4.2 mg of iron per 100 g.CALAMARES:Calamares are a type of seafood that contains 1.7 mg of iron per 100 g.PULPO:Pulpo is a type of seafood that contains 1.7 mg of iron per 100 g.ACELGAS:Acelgas are leafy greens that contain 3.1 mg of iron per 100 g.VACUNO:Vacuno refers to beef that contains 2.5 mg of iron per 100 g.CERDO:Cerdo refers to pork that contains 1.5 mg of iron per 100 g.PIPAS:Pipas are seeds that contain 6.3 mg of iron per 100 g.NUTRICI√ìN("ENTITY":FL√öOR:Fluorine is recommended to prevent dental caries and is primarily sourced from drinking water.PIR√ÅMIDES DE LA ALIMENTACI√ìN:Food pyramids are used as educational models to establish general dietary recommendations.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN MAGNESIO:Foods rich in magnesium include green leafy vegetables, cereals, and nuts, which are important for various bodily functions.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN ZINC:Foods rich in zinc include red meat and seafood, which are essential for growth and immune function.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN SELENIO:Foods rich in selenium include fish, meat, and cereals, which help protect against oxidative stress.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN FL√öOR:Fluorine is primarily found in drinking water and is important for dental health.TABLAS 17 Y 18:Tablas 17 y 18 provide a guide for the recommended frequencies of food consumption and portion sizes according to age.TABLA 19:Tabla 19 lists the main food sources of essential nutrients.A